## Refactor Checklist

| # | Refactor | Why it matters | How to do it (quick example) |
|---|----------|----------------|------------------------------|
| 1 | **Move all imports to a dedicated “route‑index” file** | The file is a huge 200+ line “monster”.  Importing every route here makes the file hard to scan and forces the linter to load a huge number of modules even if the server is started in a different mode (e.g. only the health‑check endpoint for CI). | `routes/index.ts`<br>`export * from './health.js'`<br>`export * from './models.js'`<br>In `server.ts` just `import * as routes from './routes/index.js'` |
| 2 | **Group route registrations into logical arrays** | Repeated `app.use('/api/generate', generateRouter)` etc. is error‑prone and hard to maintain.  A single loop keeps the order explicit and allows dynamic enabling/disabling. | ```ts<br>const routes: Array<{ path: string; router: Router }> = [\n  { path: '/api/health', router: routes.health },\n  { path: '/api/models', router: routes.models },\n  …\n];\nroutes.forEach(r => app.use(r.path, r.router));``` |
| 3 | **Eliminate duplicate mounts** | `/api/generate`, `/api/orchestrator` and the “/” + “/api” mounts for **ollamaCompatRouter** will clash.  The second one silently overrides the first or creates routing ambiguity. | Keep one mount per prefix.<br>For example: `app.use('/api/generate', routes.generate);` and drop the `app.use('/', ollamaCompatRouter)` line. |
| 4 | **Use environment‑controlled feature flags** | Some services (Performance‑RAG, MongoDB‑RAG‑Adapter) should only run in production.  Having hard‑coded `initService('…')` calls in the same file makes it impossible to spin the app in a “thin” mode.  Feature flags also help CI to avoid long startup times. | ```ts<br>const enabled = process.env.ENABLE_PERFORMANCE_RAG === 'true';\nif (enabled) initService('Performance RAG', … );``` |
| 5 | **Replace global boolean flags with a `Set<string>`** | `isRAGServiceManagerInitialized`, `isPerformanceRAGInitialized`, `isMongoDBRAGAdapterInitialized` are all the same pattern.  A single `Set` (or Map) makes idempotency obvious and eliminates duplicate code. | ```ts<br>const initialized = new Set<string>();\nfunction ensure(name: string, fn: () => Promise<void>) {\n  if (initialized.has(name)) return;\n  initialized.add(name);\n  fn();\n}\nensure('RAG', async () => { … });``` |
| 6 | **Remove unused middleware imports** | `jsonParser`, `corsMiddleware`, `requestLogger` are never used.  Each unused import adds to startup time and to the cognitive load of anyone reading the file. | Delete the import lines and any code that references them.  If you still want a reusable `jsonParser` simply replace `express.json({ limit: '10mb' })` with that. |
| 7 | **Normalize the JSON body‑parser** | You call `express.json()` directly and also import a `jsonParser`.  If the custom parser contains additional logic (e.g. request‑logging, error‑normalisation) you should use it consistently. | ```ts<br>app.use(routes.jsonParser);```<br>or just keep the Express default and remove the unused import. |
| 8 | **Simplify the error‑handler** | The inline error‑handler is a full‑stack conditional that is duplicated elsewhere (e.g. `initService` logs “already initialized”).  A tiny, type‑safe error‑middleware keeps the server file clean. | ```ts<br>function errorHandler(err: any, _req: Request, res: Response, _next: NextFunction) {\n  const status = err.status || 500;\n  res.status(status).json({ error: err.message || 'Internal Server Error' });\n}\napp.use(errorHandler);\n``` |
| 9 | **Resolve the `ragReadyPromise` once the RAG router is mounted** | The current code never calls `ragReadyResolve()`, so the promise never resolves.  Downstream code that `await`s `ragReadyPromise` will hang forever. | ```ts<br>initService('RAG System', async () => {\n  …\n  app.use('/api/rag', ragRouter);\n  isRAGServiceManagerInitialized = true;\n  ragReadyResolve?.();   // ← add this line\n});``` |
| 10 | **Centralise MongoDB connection and other “must‑run‑first” services** | The startup logic is mixed in the same file.  Pulling DB‑connect, server‑registry, tool registration, and the various `initService` calls into a dedicated `services‑init.ts` file clarifies the startup sequence and makes unit‑testing easier. | ```ts<br>import { initAllServices } from './services-init.js';\nawait initAllServices();``` |
| 11 | **Avoid circular imports** | `initService` imports `getOrchestratorInstance` again after it’s already been imported.  Circular imports are fragile in Node / ES‑modules and make stack‑traces hard to read.  Import only where you need the value. | Delete the earlier `import { getOrchestratorInstance } from './orchestrator-instance.js';` that is never used. |
| 12 | **Rename ambiguous router paths** | `/api/orchestrator` is used twice (once for `orchestratorRouter` and once for `modelMapRouter`).  That creates a naming collision and is hard to reason about.  Give each router a unique prefix or merge them in a single orchestrator router. | ```ts<br>app.use('/api/orchestrator', orchestratorRouter);\napp.use('/api/memory', modelMapRouter);``` |
| 13 | **Remove duplicate route mounts** | `app.use('/', ollamaCompatRouter);` and `app.use('/api', ollamaCompatRouter);` serve the same content under two different URLs.  This can lead to unexpected behaviours (e.g. `/` vs `/api`).  Keep a single, clear base path. | Delete the `app.use('/', ollamaCompatRouter);` line. |
| 14 | **Replace hard‑coded `/api` prefixes with constants** | Having a dozen `app.use('/api', …)` blocks scatters the base path all over the file.  A constant makes the prefix discoverable and allows other services (e.g. a CDN or a reverse proxy) to change the path without touching the server code. | ```ts<br>const API = '/api';\napp.use(`${API}/health`, routes.health);\napp.use(`${API}/rag`, ragRouter);``` |
| 15 | **Extract dynamic import logic into helper functions** | Several `initService` blocks perform the same “import‑then‑use” pattern.  Wrapping that in a small helper removes duplication. | ```ts<br>async function mountDynamic(path: string, importFn: () => Promise<{ default: Router }>) {\n  const mod = await importFn();\n  app.use(path, mod.default);\n}\n\nmountDynamic('/api/memory', () => import('./routes/characterMemory.js'));\n``` |
| 16 | **Keep startup logic async‑safe** | `await startup()` is fine, but any unhandled rejection will bubble up to the event loop and cause a silent crash.  Wrap it in a top‑level `try/catch` and log the error before `process.exit(1)`. | ```ts<br>async function bootstrap() {\n  try { await startup(); }\n  catch (e) { console.error(e); process.exit(1); }\n}\nawait bootstrap();``` |
| 17 | **Add a “not‑found” handler** | Right now any request that isn’t matched by a registered router will silently return `404` with no body.  A small middleware that returns a JSON payload makes the API self‑documenting and friendly to consumers. | ```ts<br>app.use((_req, res) => {\n  res.status(404).json({ error: 'Not found' });\n});``` |
| 18 | **Move TypeScript type imports to the places they are used** | The file imports `Request`, `Response` at the top but only uses them in the error handler.  Pull them into that file or remove them entirely if you never reference the types. | Remove `import { Request, Response } from 'express';` or use them in the error‑handler type declaration. |
| 19 | **Add `tsconfig.json` strictness and linting rules** | The file mixes JavaScript (`.js`) and TypeScript (`.ts`).  Enforcing `noImplicitAny`, `strictNullChecks`, etc. will surface the real types and prevent accidental `any` in the future. | ```json\n{\n  \"compilerOptions\": {\n    \"strict\": true,\n    \"noImplicitAny\": true\n  }\n}\n``` |
| 20 | **Document the startup sequence** | The `startup()` function is a mix of DB connect, server registry, tools, and RAG init.  A separate README or in‑code comment that outlines the order makes onboarding easier. | Add a comment block at the top of `server.ts` explaining: 1) DB, 2) Registry, 3) Tools, 4) Services (via `initService`). |

---

## Concrete “Before / After” Excerpts

### 1.  Route‑Index (`routes/index.ts`)

```ts
//  routes/index.ts
export { default as health }          from './health.js';
export { default as models }          from './models.js';
export { default as inference }       from './inference.js';     // <-- create a router file that aggregates infer/llm
export { default as rag }             from './rag/index.js';
export { default as orchestrator }    from './orchestrator/index.js';
export { default as memory }          from './memory/index.js';
export { default as user }            from './user/index.js';
export { default as queue }           from './queue.js';
// … add any other router exports here
```

### 2.  Route Registration Loop (`server.ts`)

```ts
import express, { Request, Response, NextFunction } from 'express';
import * as routes from './routes/index.js';
import { initService } from './utils/initService.js';
import { sharedDatabaseConnection } from './config/db.js';
import { logger } from './utils/logger.js';

const API = '/api';
const app = express();

app.use(express.json({ limit: '10mb' }));   // or routes.jsonParser

// --- Mount all routers ---------------------------------------------------
const ROUTERS = [
  { path: `${API}/health`,      router: routes.health },
  { path: `${API}/models`,      router: routes.models },
  { path: `${API}/inference`,   router: routes.inference },
  { path: `${API}/rag`,         router: routes.rag },
  { path: `${API}/orchestrator`,router: routes.orchestrator },
  { path: `${API}/memory`,      router: routes.memory },
  { path: `${API}/queue`,       router: routes.queue },
  { path: `${API}/user`,        router: routes.user },
  // … any additional mounts
];

ROUTERS.forEach(({ path, router }) => app.use(path, router));

// --- Error handling ------------------------------------------------------
function errorHandler(err: any, _req: Request, res: Response, _next: NextFunction) {
  const status = err.status ?? 500;
  logger.error(err);
  res.status(status).json({ error: err.message ?? 'Internal Server Error' });
}
app.use(errorHandler);

// --- 404 fallback --------------------------------------------------------
app.use((_req, res) => res.status(404).json({ error: 'Not found' }));
```

### 3.  Service Idempotency (`server.ts`)

```ts
const initialized = new Set<string>();

function ensure(name: string, fn: () => Promise<void>) {
  if (initialized.has(name)) return;
  initialized.add(name);
  fn();
}

ensure('RAG', async () => {
  const { default: ragRouter } = await import('./routes/rag.js');
  app.use(`${API}/rag`, ragRouter);
  ragReadyResolve?.();
});
```

### 4.  Startup Sequence (`services-init.ts`)

```ts
import { initService } from './utils/initService.js';
import { sharedDatabaseConnection } from './config/db.js';
import { initRegistry } from './services/orchestrator/registry.js';
import { registerTools } from './services/tools.js';

export async function initAllServices() {
  await sharedDatabaseConnection.connect();        // 1) DB
  await initRegistry();                             // 2) Server registry
  await registerTools();                            // 3) Register command‑line tools

  initService('Performance RAG', async () => { ... });
  initService('MongoDB‑RAG‑Adapter', async () => { ... });
  // … other services
}
```

---

## Summary

- **One router per base path** → fewer collisions.
- **Feature flags** → Flexible deployment.
- **Set for initialization** → Less duplication and clearer intent.
- **Dedicated startup module** → Easier testing and maintenance.
- **Consistent error handling & not‑found** → Cleaner API surface.

Adopting even a subset of these changes (especially the route‑index and registration loop) dramatically reduces the length and complexity of the file, speeds up start‑up, and makes future maintenance a lot easier.  Feel free to cherry‑pick the changes that fit your team’s workflow. Happy refactoring!

