## Refactor Overview

Below is a **road‑map** you can follow to turn the current one‑file module into a clean, maintainable, testable library.

| Area | What to change | Why |
|------|----------------|-----|
| **File layout** | Split the file into 4–5 files: <br> 1️⃣ `constants.ts` – test texts, thresholds, etc. <br> 2️⃣ `utils.ts` – common helpers (`fetchWithTimeout`, `measureTime`, `rubricBuilder`, `clampScore`, etc.) <br> 3️⃣ `metrics.ts` – types and calculation helpers (`calcStdDev`, `calcCV`, `calcSimilarityScore`, etc.) <br> 4️⃣ `embeddings.ts` – the three public APIs (`evaluateEmbeddingQuality`, `evaluateEmbeddingSpeed`, `detectEmbeddingModel`) <br> 5️⃣ `index.ts` – re‑export public API | Keeps concerns separate, simplifies imports, and makes the file sizes reasonable. |
| **Configuration** | Move *all* literals (timeouts, threshold values, number of test texts, etc.) into `constants.ts`. Use an exported `Config` interface so that tests can override them. | Avoid “magic numbers”; tests can inject different values; changes are localised. |
| **API wrapper** | Create a single `apiClient.ts` that exports `postJSON(url, body, timeoutMs)` returning `{ok: boolean, status: number, json?: any}`. Internally it uses `AbortController`. | Re‑uses the same error‑handling pattern everywhere; makes unit‑testing of the network layer trivial. |
| **Rubric builder** | Replace string concatenation with a `Rubric` helper class that pushes lines and finally returns the joined string. | Makes rubric generation readable and prevents accidental missing new‑lines or duplicated “Final score” lines. |
| **Score calculation** | Extract the logic that turns a numeric value into a `score` and `rubric` into a function `calculateScore(score, rubricLines, finalWeight = 1)`. | All scoring rules live in one place; easier to tweak. |
| **Timing** | Use `performance.now()` instead of `Date.now()` for sub‑millisecond precision. Wrap calls with a `timeIt(async () => { … })` helper that returns the elapsed time and optionally the result. | Removes repeated `Date.now()` boilerplate; more accurate. |
| **Similarity calculation** | Put the cosine‑similarity / average‑similarity logic into a separate function (`averageCosineSimilarity(vectors)`), and a function that turns a pair of text‑pairs into a score. | Unit‑testable and reusable in other benchmarks (e.g. clustering). |
| **Error handling** | All `try/catch` blocks should funnel into a single helper `handleError(operation: string, err: unknown)` that logs and returns a standard error result. | Removes duplicated error‑handling boilerplate. |
| **Type safety** | • Add `enum BenchmarkType` for the `type` field. <br>• Replace `any` from `fetch` with proper typings (`Response`). <br>• Make `detectEmbeddingModel` return a strict `DetectionResult` interface. | Stronger compile‑time guarantees and better IDE support. |
| **Testing** | Create a test‑suite that mocks `fetch` and the logger. Verify: <br>• Correct scores for mocked fast/slow embeddings. <br>• Correct rubric strings. <br>• Correct detection of embedding vs. generate models. | Confidence that changes don’t break behaviour. |
| **Documentation** | Use JSDoc on every exported function; add a README that explains the scoring rubric. | Easier onboarding for contributors. |
| **Performance** | Use `Promise.all` for speed tests where the order of results isn’t important. | Avoids unnecessary request queuing. |
| **Concurrency** | For `detectEmbeddingModel` use a single `AbortController` and pass its signal to *both* requests. | Prevents “unreplied” requests when the first one times out. |

Below are concrete snippets that illustrate how the refactor would look.  
Feel free to adapt the exact file structure to your project – the key is *separating concerns* and *centralising reusable logic*.

