| 8 | **Handle errors centrally** | If one `ensureNode` call fails, the whole batch fails – which is usually what you want. If you need resilience, add a try/catch around the `map` pipeline. | ```ts<br>try {<br>  const nodes = await Promise.all(performances.map(createNode));<br>} catch (err) {<br>  /* log / re‑throw */<br>}<br>``` |
| 9 | **Use `const` everywhere** | Reinforces immutability. | Replace `let nodes: Node[] = []` with `const nodes = await …` in the new version. |
|10 | **Export the helper if useful elsewhere** | If you later want to create a single node from a performance object in another file, the helper is ready to use. | ```ts<br>export { createNode };<br>``` |

---

#### Refactored Code

```ts
import { ensureNode } from 'shared/node/nodeService.js';
import { Node } from 'shared/types/index.js';

/** Constant node type – change once if needed. */
const NODE_TYPE = 'model-performance';

/**
 * Payload used to create a node for a model’s performance.
 */
export interface PerformanceInput {
  /** Identifier of the model. */
  modelId: string;
  /** Performance metric value. */
  performance: number;
  /** Optional free‑form metadata. */
  metadata?: Record<string, unknown>;
}

/**
 * Builds the arguments required by {@link ensureNode} for a single performance record.
 * @param performance - A single performance snapshot.
 * @returns A promise that resolves with the created {@link Node}.
 */
function createNode({ modelId, performance, metadata = {} }: PerformanceInput): Promise<Node> {
  // Guard against non‑object metadata being passed inadvertently.
  const safeMetadata =
    typeof metadata === 'object' && metadata !== null ? metadata : {};

  return ensureNode({
    type: NODE_TYPE,
    title: `Performance for ${modelId}`,
    metadata: { performance, ...safeMetadata },
  });
}

/**
 * Creates nodes for model performance based on the provided data.
 *
 * @param performances - Array of performance data to create nodes for.
 * @returns Promise resolving to an array of created nodes.
 */
export async function createModelPerformanceNodes(
  performances: PerformanceInput[]
): Promise<Node[]> {
  // Execute all node creations in parallel for speed.
  return await Promise.all(performances.map(createNode));
}
```

---

### Why the changes help

| Aspect | Improvement |
|--------|-------------|
| **Type safety** | `PerformanceInput` enforces correct shape and makes `metadata` optional. |
| **Performance** | `Promise.all` runs calls concurrently, dramatically reducing wall‑time for large batches. |
| **Readability** | Extracted helper (`createNode`) and constants keep the main function concise and self‑documenting. |
| **Maintainability** | All payload construction logic lives in one place; changing the node shape requires edits in only one function. |
| **Error handling** | Centralised `try/catch` in callers (if desired) keeps the function pure; you can easily add resilience later. |
| **Future‑proofing** | Exporting `createNode` and defining an interface make the code base easier to extend (e.g., unit tests, reuse in other modules). |

If your project never performs many node creations in a single batch or if you intentionally want them sequential, the original loop is fine. However, the refactored version follows modern best practices for async code in TypeScript and will scale better as your dataset grows.

