refactorSuggestion:
Below are concrete, *actionable* refactorings that will make this handler easier to read, easier to test, and safer to maintain.  
I’ve grouped them into “What to change” + “Why it matters” + “How to do it”.  
Feel free to cherry‑pick the ones that fit your project style.

---

## 1. Give the request body a type

```ts
// src/api/types.ts
export interface DeleteModelRequest {
  name: string;
}
```

```ts
// in the handler file
import type { DeleteModelRequest } from '../types';
```

**Why?**  
* Typescript will catch typos and missing fields at compile time.  
* IDEs will provide better autocompletion.

---

## 2. Extract the “delete on a server” logic into a helper

```ts
// src/services/modelDeletion.ts
import fetch from 'node-fetch';           // <-- static import, no dynamic import per request
import type { AIServer } from '../../orchestrator.js';

export async function deleteModelFromServer(
  server: AIServer,
  name: string
): Promise<any> {
  const resp = await fetch(`${server.url}/api/delete`, {
    method: 'DELETE',
    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
    body: JSON.stringify({ name }),
  });

  if (!resp.ok) {
    const rawText = await resp.text();

    if (resp.status === 404) {
      throw new Error(`Model '${name}' not found on server ${server.id}`);
    }
    throw new Error(`Server ${server.id} returned status ${resp.status}: ${rawText}`);
  }

  return resp.json();
}
```

**Why?**  
* Keeps the handler focused on orchestration, not on HTTP plumbing.  
* Allows unit‑testing `deleteModelFromServer` in isolation.  
* No repeated dynamic imports → better performance and cleaner bundle.

---

## 3. Add a small service layer on the orchestrator

```ts
// src/orchestrator.js (pseudo‑code)
export async function deleteModel(name: string): Promise<any> {
  return await this.tryRequestWithFailover(name, (server) =>
    deleteModelFromServer(server, name)
  );
}
```

```ts
// in the handler
const orchestrator = await getOrchestrator(req);
const result = await orchestrator.deleteModel(name);
```

**Why?**  
* The orchestration logic stays in one place.  
* The handler becomes a thin glue between HTTP and business logic.

---

## 4. Consolidate error handling into a single mapper

```ts
// src/services/errorMapper.ts
export function mapDeleteModelError(
  err: unknown,
  name: string
): { status: number; body: Record<string, string> } {
  if (!(err instanceof Error)) {
    return { status: 500, body: { error: 'Internal server error' } };
  }

  const message = err.message;

  if (message.includes('No healthy servers')) {
    return { status: 404, body: { error: `Model '${name}' not found on any healthy server` } };
  }

  if (message.includes('not found')) {
    return { status: 404, body: { error: message } };
  }

  // Fallback
  return { status: 500, body: { error: 'Failed to delete model' } };
}
```

**Why?**  
* Removes duplicated `if/else` chains from the handler.  
* Makes it trivial to adjust the mapping in one place later.  
* Keeps the handler focused on flow control.

---

## 5. Replace console.* with a proper logger

```ts
import logger from '../logging';  // e.g. winston, pino, etc.
```

```ts
// instead of console.error
logger.error('[ollama-delete] Error:', err);
```

**Why?**  
* Consistent log format, ability to switch log level, external tooling integration.  

---

## 6. Clean‑up imports & avoid unused variables

```ts
import type { Request, Response, NextFunction } from 'express';
import type { AIServer } from '../../orchestrator.js';
import {
  handleCompatibilityError,
  sendErrorResponse,
  validateRequiredFields,
  getOrchestrator,
} from '../shared/index.js';
import { DeleteModelRequest } from '../types';
import { deleteModelFromServer } from '../services/modelDeletion';
import { mapDeleteModelError } from '../services/errorMapper';
```

Remove any unused imports (e.g. if `handleCompatibilityError` is no longer needed after moving error logic into `mapDeleteModelError`).

---

## 7. Refactor the handler itself

```ts
export async function deleteHandler(
  req: Request,
  res: Response,
  next: NextFunction
): Promise<void> {
  try {
    // 1️⃣ Validate
    const validation = validateRequiredFields(req.body, ['name']);
    if (!validation.isValid) {
      return sendErrorResponse(
        res,
        400,
        `Missing required fields: ${validation.missing.join(', ')}`,
        'invalid_request_error'
      );
    }

    const { name } = req.body as DeleteModelRequest;

    // 2️⃣ Execute business logic
    const orchestrator = await getOrchestrator(req);
    const result = await orchestrator.deleteModel(name);

    // 3️⃣ Invalidate cache
    await orchestrator.updateAllStatusAndCache();

    // 4️⃣ Success response
    return res.json(result ?? { status: 'success' });

  } catch (err) {
    const { status, body } = mapDeleteModelError(err, req.body?.name ?? '');
    return res.status(status).json(body);
  }
}
```

**Why?**  

| Step | Benefit |
|------|---------|
| **1️⃣ Validation** | Early return keeps the rest of the function “happy”. |
| **2️⃣ Business logic** | Delegates to the orchestrator, making the handler a thin wrapper. |
| **3️⃣ Cache** | Explicitly documented side‑effect. |
| **4️⃣ Error handling** | Centralised mapping – no nested `if/else`. |

---

