refactorSuggestion:
Below are concrete, small‑to‑medium refactorings that will make the handler easier to read, test, and evolve.  
All changes are optional, but adopting most of them will give you a cleaner, more maintainable module.

---

## 1. Split the logic into self‑contained helper functions

The handler is doing three distinct things:

| Task | Suggested helper |
|------|------------------|
| 1. **Validate the request body** | `validateEmbeddingsRequest(req.body)` |
| 2. **Ask the orchestrator to fetch embeddings** | `fetchEmbeddingsFromServer(orchestrator, model, inputs)` |
| 3. **Return or surface errors** | keep inside the handler or move into a dedicated error‑handler**

Putting each task in its own function:

```ts
// src/routes/embeddings.ts
export async function embeddingsHandler(req: Request, res: Response): Promise<void> {
    const { model, inputs } = validateEmbeddingsRequest(req.body);

    const orchestrator = await getOrchestrator(req);

    try {
        const result = await fetchEmbeddingsFromServer(orchestrator, model, inputs);
        res.json(result);
    } catch (error) {
        handleEmbeddingsError(res, error);
    }
}
```

### a. Request validation

```ts
// src/utils/validation.ts
export interface EmbeddingsRequest {
    model: string;
    input: string | string[];
}

export function validateEmbeddingsRequest(body: unknown): { model: string; inputs: string[] } {
    const validation = validateRequiredFields(body as any, ['model', 'input']);

    if (!validation.isValid) {
        const msg = `Missing required fields: ${validation.missing.join(', ')}`;
        throw new BadRequestError(msg, 'invalid_request_error');
    }

    const { model, input } = body as EmbeddingsRequest;
    return {
        model,
        inputs: Array.isArray(input) ? input : [input],
    };
}
```

> **Why?**  
> * The handler no longer needs to juggle validation logic.  
> * A dedicated error class (`BadRequestError`) makes error handling consistent.

### b. Fetching embeddings

```ts
// src/utils/embeddings.ts
import fetch from 'node-fetch';

export async function fetchEmbeddingsFromServer(
    orchestrator: Orchestrator,
    model: string,
    inputs: string[],
): Promise<{ model: string; embeddings: number[][] }> {
    return orchestrator.tryRequestWithFailover(model, async (server: AIServer) => {
        const serverUrl = server.url;

        const embeddings = await Promise.all(
            inputs.map(async (prompt) => {
                const resp = await fetch(`${serverUrl}/api/embed`, {
                    method: 'POST',
                    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
                    body: JSON.stringify({ model, prompt }),
                });

                if (!resp.ok) {
                    const raw = await resp.text();
                    logger.error(`[embeddings] Server ${server.id} failed: ${resp.status} – ${raw}`);
                    throw new ServerError(server.id, resp.status, raw);
                }

                const { embedding } = (await resp.json()) as { embedding: number[] };
                return embedding;
            }),
        );

        return { model, embeddings };
    });
}
```

> **Why?**  
> * Keeps the handler focused on orchestration, not on HTTP mechanics.  
> * A dedicated `ServerError` class (see below) lets us identify server‑specific failures later.

---

## 2. Use explicit error types

Instead of inspecting `error.message`, throw and catch concrete error classes.  

```ts
// src/errors.ts
export class BadRequestError extends Error {
    public readonly code: string;
    constructor(message: string, code: string) {
        super(message);
        this.name = 'BadRequestError';
        this.code = code;
    }
}

export class ServerError extends Error {
    public readonly serverId: string;
    public readonly status: number;
    constructor(serverId: string, status: number, raw: string) {
        super(`Server ${serverId} returned ${status}: ${raw}`);
        this.name = 'ServerError';
        this.serverId = serverId;
        this.status = status;
    }
}

export class NoHealthyServersError extends Error {
    constructor() {
        super('No healthy servers available for embedding model');
        this.name = 'NoHealthyServersError';
    }
}
```

Now the handler can be:

```ts
// src/routes/embeddings.ts
function handleEmbeddingsError(res: Response, err: unknown): void {
    if (err instanceof BadRequestError) {
        sendErrorResponse(res, 400, err.message, err.code);
    } else if (err instanceof NoHealthyServersError) {
        sendErrorResponse(res, 503, err.message, 'service_unavailable');
    } else if (err instanceof ServerError) {
        // ServerError is already logged inside the fetch helper
        handleCompatibilityError(res, err, 'Failed to generate embeddings');
    } else {
        // generic fallback
        console.error('[embeddings] Unexpected error:', err);
        handleCompatibilityError(res, err, 'Failed to generate embeddings');
    }
}
```

**Benefits**

* No fragile `error.message.includes(...)` checks.  
* Easier to unit‑test each error branch.  
* The orchestrator can now `throw new NoHealthyServersError()` instead of a plain string.

---

## 3. Remove the unused `next` parameter

If you are handling all errors inside the handler, you don’t need `next`.  
If you prefer to let Express’s error middleware do the heavy lifting, simply call `next(error)` in the catch block and drop the `res.json(result)` branch from the error handling logic.

```ts
export async function embeddingsHandler(req: Request, res: Response, next: NextFunction): Promise<void> {
    try {
        ...
        res.json(result);
    } catch (err) {
        next(err); // Express will pick up the error
    }
}
```

---

## 4. Adopt a structured logger

Replace `console.error` with a real logger so you can control log levels, format, and transports.

```ts
// src/utils/logger.ts
import pino from 'pino';
export const logger = pino({ level: process.env.LOG_LEVEL ?? 'info' });
```

Use `logger.error(...)` inside the fetch helper and anywhere else you need logging.

---

## 5. Optional – Use the global `fetch` (Node 18+)

If your runtime already has `fetch`, you can avoid importing `node-fetch`:

```ts
const fetchFn: typeof fetch =
    typeof globalThis.fetch === 'function'
        ? globalThis.fetch.bind(globalThis)
        : (await import('node-fetch')).default;
```

But the dynamic import approach is fine; it simply makes the dependency explicit.

---

## 6. Final “clean” skeleton

```ts
// src/routes/embeddings.ts
import { Request, Response, NextFunction } from 'express';
import { getOrchestrator } from '../shared/index.js';
import {
    validateEmbeddingsRequest,
    fetchEmbeddingsFromServer,
} from '../utils/embeddings.js';
import { handleEmbeddingsError } from '../utils/errorHandler.js';

export async function embeddingsHandler(
    req: Request,
    res: Response,
    next: NextFunction,
): Promise<void> {
    try {
        const { model, inputs } = validateEmbeddingsRequest(req.body);
        const orchestrator = await getOrchestrator(req);
        const result = await fetchEmbeddingsFromServer(orchestrator, model, inputs);
        res.json(result);
    } catch (err) {
        // either use next(err) if you have global error middleware
        // or keep the local handler
        handleEmbeddingsError(res, err);
    }
}
```

---

## 7. Summary of the changes

| Refactor | What it improves |
|----------|------------------|
| Extracted request validation | Keeps the handler focused; easier unit tests |
| Moved fetch logic to helper | Separates concerns, reuse across routes |
| Created explicit error classes | Predictable error handling, no string parsing |
| Removed unused `next` | Clean signature, less cognitive load |
| Switched to a structured logger | Consistent logging, better production observability |
| Optional: use global `fetch` | Smaller bundle if available |

Apply these changes gradually. If you’re on a team, a small PR that introduces the helper functions and error classes will make a huge difference in readability and maintainability. If no refactoring is needed, just keep the current code as is—though I think the above suggestions will pay off quickly.

