refactorSuggestion:
Below are a few concrete ideas that can help tighten up this barrel‑file, make it easier to reason about, and reduce the risk of subtle bugs in the future.

| # | Refactoring Idea | Why it matters | How to implement (code snippets) |
|---|------------------|----------------|----------------------------------|
| 1 | **Avoid duplicating re‑exports** | The same module (`./embeddings.js`) is re‑exported twice – once as a wildcard and once for a single handler. This makes it hard to know what the public API actually is and can lead to circular‑dependency surprises. | ```ts\n// Remove the first line that re‑exports everything from ./embeddings.js\n// Keep only the specific named export that you really need.\nexport { embeddingsHandler as handleEmbeddings } from './embeddings.js';\n``` |
| 2 | **Move the “full” re‑exports into a dedicated sub‑folder** | When a module intentionally re‑exports everything (`export * from './info.js'`) you’re effectively creating an implicit public API contract. Keep that contract in one place so that users can see *all* the exported symbols at a glance. | Create `src/api/` and inside it: <br>**index.ts**: `export * from './models.js'; export * from './info.js'; …` <br>Then in the root barrel: `<br>export * from './api';` |
| 3 | **Namespace the handlers** | A mix of generic names (`handleCreate`, `handleDelete`) and more specific names (`handleChat`, `handleGenerate`) can be confusing. Namespace them by operation (e.g. `model`, `info`, `chat`, `blob`) so that callers see a clear hierarchy. | ```ts\nexport * as model from './models.js';\nexport * as info from './info.js';\nexport * as chat from './chat.js';\nexport * as blob from './blobs.js';\n``` |
| 4 | **Add JSDoc or TS type annotations** | Right now the file is just a series of export statements. Adding minimal documentation (or type‑exports) helps IDEs surface the right API and reduces the cognitive load when a developer looks at the file for the first time. | ```ts\n/**\n * Exported handler for creating a model.\n */\nexport { handleCreate } from './models.js';\n``` |
| 5 | **Use a single source of truth for handler names** | If you decide to rename `handleEmbed` to `handleEmbeddingsParallel`, you have to touch this file *and* the implementation. Keep a small mapping table so that the barrel is the only place you need to update. | ```ts\nconst handlers = {\n  create: 'handleCreate',\n  convert: 'handleConvert',\n  stop: 'handleStop',\n  embedParallel: 'embedHandlerParallel',\n  …\n};\n\nexport const { create, … } = handlers;\n``` |
| 6 | **Group related exports together** | Having every export scattered across the file makes it hard to scan. Group by domain: models, info, chat, blob, etc. | ```ts\n// -- MODEL HANDLERS ---------------------------------\nexport { handleCreate, handleConvert, handleStop } from './models.js';\n// -- INFO HANDLERS -----------------------------------\nexport { handleVersion, … } from './info.js';\n// -- CHAT HANDLERS -----------------------------------\nexport { handleChat, handleGenerate } from './chat.js';\n``` |
| 7 | **Make the file explicit about its role** | The comment says “Re-exports all Ollama‑specific handlers and utilities”. That is useful, but adding a short `@module` JSDoc can help tooling. | ```ts\n/**\n * @module ollama/api\n * Barrel that re‑exports the public API of the Ollama SDK.\n */\n``` |
| 8 | **Optional – migrate to ES‑module “named exports” only** | If you’re not using TypeScript, consider removing `export *` and listing the exact names you want to expose. This prevents accidental leaking of private helpers. | ```ts\nexport { handleCreate, handleConvert, handleStop } from './models.js';\n// … etc.\n``` |

---

### Refactored Example

Below is a clean, minimal example that incorporates most of the above suggestions. Adjust it to match your actual module layout and naming conventions.

```ts
/**
 * @module ollama/api
 * Barrel that re‑exports the public API of the Ollama SDK.
 *
 * The API is grouped by domain so that consumers can import what they need
 * in a single line, e.g.:
 *
 *   import { model, chat, blob } from 'ollama/api';
 *
 * All symbols are re‑exported with explicit names to avoid accidental leaks.
 */

 // ---------------------- MODEL HANDLERS ----------------------
export {
  handleCreate,
  handleConvert,
  handleStop,
} from './models.js';

// ---------------------- INFO HANDLERS ----------------------
export {
  handleVersion,
  handlePs,
  handleTags,
  handleShowGet,
  handleShow,
} from './info.js';

// ---------------------- CHAT HANDLERS ----------------------
export { handleChat, handleGenerate } from './chat.js';

// ---------------------- BLOB HANDLERS ----------------------
export {
  blobHeadHandler as handleBlobHead,
  blobPostHandler as handleBlobPost,
} from './blobs.js';

// ---------------------- EMBEDDINGS HANDLERS ----------------
export { embeddingsHandler as handleEmbeddings } from './embeddings.js';
export { embedHandlerParallel as handleEmbed } from './embed-parallel.js';

// ---------------------- OTHER ENDPOINTS --------------------
export { copyHandler as handleCopy } from './copy.js';
export { deleteHandler as handleDelete } from './delete.js';
export { pullHandler as handlePull } from './pull.js';
export { pushHandler as handlePush } from './push.js';
```

> **Bottom line** – The key is consistency. Pick a single re‑export strategy (e.g. namespace vs. flat) and stick to it. Keep the barrel file intentionally short, well‑commented, and free of accidental duplicate exports. That will make the API much easier to maintain and to use.

