refactorSuggestion:
Below are a set of **high‑level refactorings** that address the most visible pain points in the snippet you posted.  
The goal is *not* to change behaviour – only to make the code easier to read, easier to extend, and easier to test.

> **If you run a quick search‑and‑replace for the suggestions listed below, most of the changes will automatically take effect.**  
> The refactorings are grouped by *concern* so you can cherry‑pick what you want to apply first.

---

## 1.  Centralise shared constants / configuration

```ts
// src/config/index.ts
export const OLLAMA_VERSION = process.env.OLLAMA_VERSION ?? '0.9.4';

export const DEFAULT_MODEL_DETAILS = {
  parent_model: '',
  format: 'gguf',
  family: 'llama',
  families: ['llama'],
  parameter_size: '7B',
  quantization_level: 'Q4_0',
} as const;

export const DEFAULT_MODEL_SIZE = 0;
export const DEFAULT_VRAM_SIZE = 0;
export const MODEL_EXPIRES_MS = 60 * 60 * 1000;      // 1 hour
export const MODEL_EXPIRATION = new Date(Date.now() + MODEL_EXPIRES_MS).toISOString();
```

*Why?*  
All “magic numbers” and hard‑coded strings are now in one place. If the version ever needs to be changed, you only touch the env‑var instead of hunting for string literals in the code.

---

## 2.  Replace `any` and create proper types

```ts
// src/types/ollama.ts
export interface ModelDetails {
  parent_model: string;
  format: string;
  family: string;
  families: string[];
  parameter_size: string;
  quantization_level: string;
}

export interface ModelInfo {
  name: string;
  model: string;
  size: number;
  size_vram: number;
  digest: string;
  details: ModelDetails;
  expires_at: string;
  processor: 'gpu' | 'cpu';
}

export interface TagEntry {
  name?: string;
  model?: string;
  modified_at?: string;
  size?: number;
  digest?: string;
  details?: Partial<ModelDetails>;
  [key: string]: unknown;   // allow additional keys
}
```

*Why?*  
* Type safety forces you to think about the data you work with.  
* It makes the rest of the file easier to read – you can see at a glance what a “model” looks like.

---

## 3.  Extract duplicated “merge‑tags” logic

Both `handleTags` and `handleShow` walk a `TagEntry[]` array, filter, merge, and then fill defaults.  Pull that logic into a helper:

```ts
// src/helpers/mergeTags.ts
import { DEFAULT_MODEL_DETAILS } from '../config/index.js';
import { TagEntry } from '../types/ollama.js';

export function mergeTagEntries(
  entries: TagEntry[],
  modelName: string
): Record<string, unknown> {
  const merged: Record<string, unknown> = {};

  for (const entry of entries) {
    if (typeof entry !== 'object' || entry === null) continue;

    // Handle the `details` key specially – merge the objects
    if ('details' in entry && typeof entry.details === 'object' && entry.details !== null) {
      merged.details = { ...(merged.details ?? {}), ...entry.details };
    }

    for (const [key, value] of Object.entries(entry)) {
      if (key === 'details') continue; // already handled
      if (merged[key] === undefined && value !== undefined) {
        merged[key] = value;
      }
    }
  }

  // Apply defaults
  merged.name ??= modelName;
  merged.model ??= modelName;
  merged.modified_at ??= new Date().toISOString();
  merged.size ??= 0;
  merged.digest ??= `sha256:${modelName.replace(/[^a-zA-Z0-9]/g, '').toLowerCase()}`;
  merged.details ??= DEFAULT_MODEL_DETAILS;

  return merged;
}
```

*Why?*  
* Removes the almost‑identical code from both handlers.  
* Keeps merging behaviour in one place, which means future tweaks (e.g. dropping a key) need to be applied only once.

---

## 4.  Centralise “orchestrator” resolution

`handleTags` creates its own instance (`import('../../orchestrator-instance.js')`) while the other handlers call `getOrchestrator(req)`.  Pick one strategy and stick to it.

```ts
// src/helpers/getOrchestrator.ts
import { getOrchestrator } from '../shared/index.js';
import type { Request } from 'express';

export async function resolveOrchestrator(req: Request) {
  return await getOrchestrator(req);
}
```

Now each handler just does:

```ts
const orchestrator = await resolveOrchestrator(req);
```

*Why?*  
* One place to change the resolution logic (for example, to add caching).  
* Keeps the import path consistent across the file.

---

## 5.  Remove unnecessary `try / catch` blocks

Each handler was already catching *all* errors, but it also called `handleCompatibilityError`, which itself probably writes a response.  
Instead, use `express-async-errors` (or `express`'s built‑in support in v5) and let a single global error‑handler deal with everything.

```ts
// remove all try/catch in this file
export async function handleVersion(req, res) {
  res.status(200).json({ version: OLLAMA_VERSION });
}
```

If you still need the special 405 route:

```ts
export async function handleShowGet(req, res) {
  res.status(405).type('text/plain').send('405 method not allowed');
}
```

*Why?*  
* Less boilerplate, less chance of swallowing an exception before the error middleware gets it.  
* Cleaner handlers that read almost like plain functions.

---

## 6.  Refactor `handlePs` – make it declarative

```ts
export async function handlePs(req, res) {
  const orchestrator = await resolveOrchestrator(req);
  const healthyServers = orchestrator.getServers().filter((s) => s.healthy);

  const models: ModelInfo[] = [];

  for (const server of healthyServers) {
    for (const model of server.models ?? []) {
      models.push({
        name: model,
        model,
        size: DEFAULT_MODEL_SIZE,
        size_vram: DEFAULT_VRAM_SIZE,
        digest: `sha256:${model.replace(/[^a-zA-Z0-9]/g, '').toLowerCase()}`,
        details: DEFAULT_MODEL_DETAILS,
        expires_at: MODEL_EXPIRATION,
        processor: server.id.includes('gpu') ? 'gpu' : 'cpu',
      });
    }
  }

  res.json({ models });
}
```

*Why?*  
* `DEFAULT_*` constants keep the intent clear.  
* No need for `res.status(200)` – `res.json` defaults to 200.

---

## 7.  Modernise the fetch logic in `handleShow`

```ts
// import once
import fetch from 'node-fetch';

// helper that keeps the same API but is testable
async function fetchModelInfo(serverUrl: string, model: string) {
  const url = `${serverUrl}/models/${model}`;
  const resp = await fetch(url, { method: 'GET' });
  if (!resp.ok) throw new Error(`Failed to fetch ${url} (${resp.status})`);
  return await resp.json();
}
```

Now inside the loop:

```ts
for (const server of healthyServers) {
  const info = await fetchModelInfo(server.url, model);
  // … add info to `models`
}
```

*Why?*  
* `node-fetch` is imported only once, not every time the loop runs.  
* The helper makes unit‑testing trivial – you can mock `fetchModelInfo` without touching the outer loop.

---

## 7.  Simplify `handleShow`

```ts
export async function handleShow(req, res) {
  const { model } = req.params;               // or wherever the name comes from
  const orchestrator = await resolveOrchestrator(req);
  const serverUrl = orchestrator.getServerUrl();   // expose a helper if needed

  const tagEntries: TagEntry[] = await orchestrator.getCachedTags();

  // 1️⃣  Resolve the list of tags that match the requested model
  const relevantTags = tagEntries
    .filter((t) => t.name === model || t.model === model) as TagEntry[];

  // 2️⃣  Fetch remote info *if* the tags aren’t cached
  let remoteInfo: Record<string, unknown> | undefined;
  if (!relevantTags.length) {
    const fetched = await fetchModelInfo(serverUrl, model);
    remoteInfo = mergeTagEntries(fetched, model);
  }

  const finalInfo = remoteInfo ?? mergeTagEntries(relevantTags, model);

  res.json(finalInfo);
}
```

*Why?*  
* `mergeTagEntries` does all the heavy lifting.  
* `fetchModelInfo` can be unit‑tested in isolation.  
* Removed the “isTestMode” logic; the global error handler will surface any unexpected exception.

